Report where the shared-cache mapping stops on the way into the kernel (enchilada instrument).

launchd is refused twice by shared_region_check_np with ENOMEM, which means the task owns a
shared region whose sr_first_mapping is still -1: no mapping was ever established. On k136 dyld
finally asked for the mapping -- the log carries map_and_slide_2 files=54 mappings=62 -- and then
nothing. The line that would follow it, setup error=, never appears, so the machine stopped either
in proc_fdlock() or inside shared_region_map_and_slide_setup().

On k138 the probe set narrowed it further: on the run that carries ready=53 the log walks all
fifty-three files and stops after "setup file 52 fd=53 mappings=2", which is printed before the
descriptor is resolved. That body has six steps that can block, and they are each reported: the
descriptor came back (file N fd ok), the ownership attribute is about to be read (attr enter),
it came back with a uid (attr done), the size is about to be read (size enter) and came back
(size done), the memory object is about to be taken (cs enter), and the loop finished (files done).

On k139 the deep run cleared all of setup -- files done error=0, setup error=0 files=54 mappings=62
-- entered vm_shared_region_map_file, laid down sixty-one of the sixty-two mappings (first mapping
idx=0..3, one of them KERN_MEMORY_PRESENT) and then stopped without ever returning. So the stop
moved inside that function and the five stages after it are now bracketed: the slide loop entry
(map file setup enter), the end of the mapping pass (map file slides done), each region_slide
call with its slide start and size (map file slide N of M), the tail that fixes up the map and
calls vm_shared_region_map_file_final (map file final enter/done), and the authenticated remap
(map file auth remap enter/kr). Those five divide the function into the copyin of the cache
header, the region_slide pager installs, pmap_trim and the auth remap, which are the only things
left that can park a boot CPU.

Bracketing every step is the only way to tell those apart from a log that dies mid-call. map enter
fires before the fd lock; map rdir reports the root directory the shared region must match once
the lock is released; setup sr reports the shared region and its root dir; setup file reports each
file walked, before its descriptor is resolved; map file brackets the call that installs the
mappings; first mapping reports the address that becomes sr_first_mapping; exit reports what the
syscall handed back to dyld; enter reports the shared region looked up for the process root.

The exit line exists because the enter/setup/mapping lines alone cannot separate two states that
look identical from the outside: dyld maps the cache and then dies before main, and dyld is
refused after the setup succeeded. Only the return value tells them apart, and it is the one
number the earlier probes never printed.

sr_first_mapping is deliberately not printed. bsd/vm/vm_unix.c only sees the forward declaration
of struct vm_shared_region, so reading the field there does not compile; k137 failed on exactly
that line, which is why setup sr stops at the pointer and the root dir.

Every per-file line is emitted fifty-three times per run, so a deep run adds about three hundred
short lines. They are still worth it: the alternative is guessing which of vnode_getattr,
ubc_getobject or the code-signature walk parks the boot CPU.

These are diagnostics only: no return value, no state and no policy changes, and each line is
prefixed NATIVE_SR so it can be filtered out of a boot log.

